From 51cfb346302f79d211ef406a0e90a6795f9daab6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 14:11:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Build and price item 1-1's free-name walk, its named
 precondition

The item names its own sub-project: a free-name walk per deferred
function, resolved against the enclosing scopes, so the enclosing
lambda's Box[] is addressable without a tree -- LambdaRewriter decides a
captured name's index FROM the tree, and a deferred body has none.
--compile-phases charged that walk at 5.4-9.9% of tree construction and
recorded the figure as a lower bound, because it counted identifiers and
resolved nothing. This builds the real one and checks the bound.

FreeNameScan tracks scopes because the answer depends on them: a var is
function-scoped and a let block-scoped, so `{ var x; } x` is bound and
`{ let x; } x` is free; parameters, catch bindings, a named function
expression's own name and hoisted declarations bind; a pattern's target
binds while its default references. A direct eval, a with or a debugger
reaches bindings never mentioned, so such a body is reported undeferrable
rather than given a bigger set.

The first implementation priced the item at up to half its own prize.
Scanning each function independently is superlinear in nesting depth --
walking a function walks every function inside it, and every enclosing
level walks it again -- which on Box2D's 978 deeply nested functions is
47.7% of body tree construction against 5.8% on the flattest corpus. The
same walk as one bottom-up pass is 6.6-12.2% everywhere, because free
names compose: a function's free set is its own references, plus its
children's, minus what it binds. Box2D goes 70.9 -> 11.4 ms.

Mandreel is the one corpus where one pass is worse, 7.8% -> 8.8%, and for
the reason this document already recorded about it: Mandreel is wide, not
deep. With almost no nesting there is nothing to re-walk and the
per-function bookkeeping is pure overhead. It is the control that says
the improvement is about depth.

So the recorded lower bound was right about the number and silent about
what decides it: 5.4-9.9% is a good estimate of a walk written the right
way and off five-fold for the obvious one.

23 fixtures, every one a pair or a negative, because a scanner returning
"every identifier" passes any test that only checks a free name is
present. Two defects came from them rather than from inspection: a
non-computed member property (o.a) read as a reference to a, and
object-literal KEYS did the same, because AstReduce walks a literal's
properties generically while routing a pattern's through
VisitObjectProperty. Either would have boxed a binding for every property
name in the program. The one-pass form is checked against an independent
naive oracle in the test project, and building that oracle found two more
bugs in the oracle, which is the cross-check working.

The deferral mechanism itself is not built. What is now known is that its
one named obstacle costs 7-12% of the work it removes, over a population
that is 84-99.7% never invoked.
